# Build context for `docker build .` — and, on Buildah/Podman, for every
# build, because Buildah has no per-Dockerfile ignore file and falls back to
# this one for `Dockerfile.test` too. So this file may not exclude anything
# `Dockerfile.test` copies, and it is held to the same rule as
# `Dockerfile.test.dockerignore`: exclude nothing the test image copies,
# and never exclude broadly and re-include with `!`. The negations are in
# the spec but did not take effect on the Podman path, and the test image
# failed on a `COPY … README.md` for a file plainly in the checkout. A test
# checks both files (ADR-104).
#
# What keeps `tests/`, the daily reports and the README out of the
# *shipped* image is `Dockerfile` itself: there is no `COPY . .`, so every
# path in that image is one somebody wrote down.

# Version control and local virtualenvs.
.git
.venv
venv

# Build and tool caches.
__pycache__
*.pyc
.pytest_cache
.mypy_cache
.ruff_cache
htmlcov
coverage.xml
.coverage

# The real secrets. `.env.example` is NOT excluded — it is test input, and
# Dockerfile.test copies it.
.env
.env.test
.env.test.example

# Prose neither image builds from. Directories, not `*.md`: that pattern
# also caught `README.md` (ADR-081), every `skills/**/SKILL.md` (ADR-038)
# and the forks' `NOTICE.md` (ADR-044).
docs

# Runtime state, never build input. Long-term memory especially: baking one
# team member's notes into the image would ship them to everyone (ADR-035,
# ADR-065).
workspace
memory
agent_memory_db
